  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As the high moves offshore, southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow will resume across the Carolinas bringing a \ngradual return of temps into the 90s as well as a jump in \nhumidity with Tds gradually working back into the 70s. Mid- \nlevel subsidence aloft will keep precipitation chances very \nsubdued through today despite the uptick in instability, \nalthough cannot completely rule out an isolated shower or two \nalong typical sound and sea breeze convergence zones. Shortwave \napproaches from the central plains on Thursday, weakening the \nridge and aiding in a slightly more favorable environment for \nisolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Friday the high continues to shift offshore and\na front sweeps south through the Mid-Atlantic region. Ahead of\nthis front, continuous warm, moist advection in southerly flow \nwill help build PWATs to near 2\". This will bring scattered to \nnumerous sea breeze showers/tstorms back in the forecast. \nMeanwhile, despite the high shifting offshore, some residual \nridging should slow down the progression of the front as it \nmoves south. Where the front ends up stalling remains a point of\nuncertainty in the forecast. However it is worth noting that\nmore models are suggesting a front draped across eastern NC\ncompared to 24 hours ago. Regardless, being 3 days out there is\nstill too much uncertainty to buy into any one outcome.  \nWherever the boundary sets up, along it training showers/thunderstorms\nare possible, and a narrow swath of deep layer shear right \nalong the front would bring both a low end hydro and severe \nthreat. The extent of the hydro and severe threat are dependent \non the frontal location and will continue to be monitored. As of\nthis afternoon, hydrologic concerns remain the primary hazard \nbut a low-end severe risk remains in the cards.\n\nThe front lifts back north Saturday into Sunday as a shortwave\nmoves through the region. This shortwave will bring slightly\nbetter chances for showers and thunderstorms this weekend. \nShear doesn't look to be impressive, but ample moisture will \npromote a marginal hydro threat. There is some uncertainty with\nhow much instability is in play Saturday, as it is dependent on\ncloud cover and associated surface heating. In a low-end CAPE \noutcome, (500-1000J/kg) we would have long skinny CAPEs that \nsuggest just a hydro threat and less frequent lightning. \nMeanwhile a high- end CAPE outcome (2000-4000 J/kg) would bring \nmore pulse- like thunderstorms capable of producing some gusty \nwinds, frequent lightning, and heavy rainfall rates. Regardless \nof how Saturday plays out, there is higher confidence on a low-\nCAPE outcome Sunday. While heavy showers and some thunderstorms \nare possible Sunday with the shortwave, the severe threat \nappears to be more muted.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A more typical summertime pattern will settle in\nagain early next week as a large high sits over the southeast.\nBased on current model outputs, we have enough moisture and \ninstability present for some afternoon/evening sea breeze \nconvection early next week, but there are no larger scale\nsynoptic features of note that would promote any additional\nforcing.",
